  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nBroad ridging over the western US coupled with troughing over the \nGreat Lakes/Northeastern US leaves our FA in northwesterly flow\naloft. In the wake of a cold front which passed through \nyesterday, temperatures today have been in the 70s area wide, a \nreprieve from recent heat. Temperatures on Wednesday will be \njust a couple of degrees warmer. Later this week, ridging over \nthe western US will build eastward. As a result, temperatures \nwill rise back into the 90s for much of the FA Friday through \nSunday. A few waves riding the periphery of the ridge will bring\nperiodic chances for thunderstorms starting Friday, lasting \ninto the weekend. Capping, timing of the waves (during peak \ndaytime heating vs overnight) and availability of moisture will\nall determine if storms actually do form any given day. Right \nnow, Friday's wave has the been potential to initiate a few \nstorms, depending on the strength of the cap. Shear profiles are\nsupportive of supercells if storms can form, with effective \nbulk shear in excess of 40 knots. \n\nThe warmest day of the week will likely be Sunday as the thermal \nridge axis situates itself over the FA. Ensemble guidance shows \nus remaining on the eastern periphery of ridging much of next \nweek as well. Eastward extend of ridging will determine how \nwarm/hot we are beyond Monday, with some guidance keeping \ntemperatures in the 90s until the last few days of July, while \nothers cool us off by the end of the forecast period into the \n80s. \n\n...Near Critical Fire Weather...\n\nRecent dry weather has caused curing of fuels in portions of the \nregion, especially in the forests of northwestern MN. RH values this \nafternoon have remained elevated (50 percent or higher) but winds \ngusting up to 25 mph could still cause any fire that starts to \nspread quickly. Therefore, an SPS is in effect into early this \nevening for near critical fire weather. Winds will be lighter on \nWednesday, although humidity will likely decrease. \n\n...Heat this Weekend...\n\nAs ridging builds east, another period of 90+ degree \ntemperatures is forecasted. The highest heat indices will be in\nsoutheastern ND, where highs are expected to be in the upper \n90s. While humidity will not be as high as with the previous \nheat wave (we do not have a good source of richer dew point air \nto advect northward), local ET contribution will likely keep dew\npoints in the upper 50s to 60s. This should maintain heat \nindices values a couple of degrees above the air temperature. \n\nTo put into context recent heat here are a few quick statistics. \nDigging into climate records a bit, Fargo's average temperatures\nfrom July 1 to 20 ranks 3rd, behind 1936 and 2012. Grand Forks \nWFO site ranks 2nd, behind only 1936. With today and tomorrow \nbeing the only days of the next 7 forecasted to have \ntemperatures near average, it appears these records will \ncontinue to hold about steady into the end of the month - and \ndepending how stubborn ridging is into next week, our monthly \naverage temperature may even climb slightly. This goes to show \nthat while we have not set a single daily high temperature \nrecord this month, the duration of heat (which will continue \nwith the upcoming stretch this weekend) has been impressive for \nour region.",
